	<key>NSAppleEventsUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Mila uses AppleEvents to paste dictated text into the active app.</string>
	<!-- Calendar: ONE feature, two keys. macOS 14+ EventKit asks for full
	     calendar access and reads NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription, but the
	     older NSCalendarsUsageDescription string is what some hosts (and older
	     tooling) still display. Both carry the same sentence, and both say what
	     the code actually does: Mila has no EventKit write call anywhere, so
	     "never creates or changes an event" is a promise it can keep. -->
	<key>NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Mila reads the event happening when you record, so a recording can be linked to it — for its name, the invite's attendees, and the invite's notes in the summary. Mila never creates or changes an event.</string>
	<key>NSCalendarsUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Mila reads the event happening when you record, so a recording can be linked to it — for its name, the invite's attendees, and the invite's notes in the summary. Mila never creates or changes an event.</string>
	<key>NSHumanReadableCopyright</key>
	<string>Written by Uri Harduf from Island</string>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Mila needs microphone access to record audio for transcription and dictation.</string>
	<key>NSPrincipalClass</key>
	<string>NSApplication</string>
	<key>NSScreenCaptureUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Mila needs screen recording permission to capture audio from apps like Zoom.</string>
	<key>NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Mila transcribes locally on your Mac; no audio is sent to the cloud.</string>
